 "findings": [
  "An upper bound on everything per user is operator-published: 5.5 kWh per daily active person per year (0.63 W) for one large social operator in 2024, up 53 % in a year and including generative AI. It is an average allocation.",
  "Nobody publishes the split by workload. The pieces that exist are from different systems (machine learning 10 to 15 % of one operator's energy in 2019-2021; storage 9 % of US data-centre electricity in 2023 and 33 % of one cloud's operational emissions). The scout's bracket for storage, analytics, ranking and advertising together: 0.5 to 5.5 kWh per daily person per year.",
  "Storing bytes at rest is cheap: about 15 kWh per logical terabyte-year after replication and cooling, so 1 to 10 GB per user is 0.015 to 0.15 kWh a year. The cost is in harvesting: derived data, the analytic warehouse, ranking and advertising.",
  "The short-run marginal saving is about zero (one cloud measured a 28 % fall in storage traffic moving storage power by 0.04 %). The avoided term is long-run capacity not built and must be labelled so.",
  "No measured energy per advertising impression exists; published figures are allocations or vendor models that disagree by a factor of eight.",
  "On the handset, apps with ads used 16 % more energy in one 2015 study of 21 apps: a true marginal, and small (0.2 to 0.3 kWh per phone-year at most).",
  "Substitution is additive on the evidence there is: in the 2021 messenger migration 26 % wanted to leave, a quarter of those managed, 0.5 % uninstalled; the median user had five messaging apps. Prior for the replaced fraction: 0.005 to 0.16."
 ],
 "not_available": [
  "bytes stored per user by any large platform",
  "any operator's energy split by serving, analytics, machine learning and storage",
  "advertising impressions per user-hour",
  "a measured energy per impression",
  "evidence on substitution after ADOPTING a private messenger (the experiments are of removal)"
 ]
}